// ============================================================================
// twin-primes-below.epher -- twin-prime pairs below 100
//
// Counts the twin-prime pairs (p, p + 2) with both members prime and both
// below 100. A packed while loop over odd candidates (guide 1.30) keeps
// the count; the loop is silent, so one labelled print shows the total.
// The eight pairs below 100 are the classic check (Hardy and Wright,
// ch. 1): (3, 5), (5, 7), (11, 13), (17, 19), (29, 31), (41, 43),
// (59, 61) and (71, 73).
//
// Source: Hardy and Wright, "An Introduction to the Theory of Numbers",
// ch. 1; whether infinitely many twin primes exist is open (the twin
// prime conjecture, ibid. ch. 1). isprime is exact, so the count is exact.
//
// Run it from a terminal:  epher "epher scripts/number-theory/primes/twin-primes-below.epher"
// In the REPL:             load epher scripts/number-theory/primes/twin-primes-below.epher
// Web/desktop:             paste the whole file into the entry, press Enter
//
// There is no knob: the bound 100 is the check value.
// ============================================================================

// ---- count twin pairs (p, p + 2) with p + 2 below 100 -------------------------
// p runs over the odd numbers 3, 5, ..., 97; p = 2 never starts a twin
// pair because p + 2 = 4 is even, so the odd scan misses nothing.
v = {3, 0}
while v[1] <= 97 do v = {v[1] + 2, v[2] + (if isprime(v[1]) and isprime(v[1] + 2) then 1 else 0)}
print("twin-prime pairs below 100:", v[2])

// ---- the pairs themselves -------------------------------------------------------
// The first members of the eight pairs, checked the same way; the loop
// collects one boolean per first member and prints them together.
for x in {3, 5, 11, 17, 29, 41, 59, 71} do isprime(x) and isprime(x + 2)
// ---- expected output ----
// = {3, 0}
// = twin-prime pairs below 100: 8
// = {true, true, true, true, true, true, true, true}
